---
import ResourceLayout from '../../components/ResourceLayout.astro';
import VideoEmbed from '../../components/VideoEmbed.astro';
import InfographicViewer from '../../components/InfographicViewer.astro';
import ExternalResource from '../../components/ExternalResource.astro';
---

<ResourceLayout
  icon="💊"
  eyebrow="Medicines and pharmacies"
  title="Medications"
  introduction="Learn how prescriptions work, what pharmacists can do, how to pay for medications, and how to use medicines safely in British Columbia."
>
  <VideoEmbed
    title="Accessing Medications in British Columbia"
    videoId="Szo2X5859ZM"
    description="Watch this short video to learn how pharmacies work, how to pay for medications, and how pharmacists can help with your healthcare."
  />

  <InfographicViewer
    title="Medication Access for Newcomers"
    description="Learn how to pay for prescriptions, what pharmacists can do, and how to safely access medications in British Columbia."
    previewSrc="/infographics/mental-health/medication-en.png"
    previewAlt="Preview of the Medication Access infographic"
    languages={[
      {
        label: 'English',
        href: '/infographics/mental-health/medication-en.png',
        lang: 'en',
      },
      {
        label: 'ਪੰਜਾਬੀ',
        href: '/infographics/mental-health/medication-pa.png',
        lang: 'pa',
      },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Trusted Resources</h2>
  <p>
    These trusted organizations can help you understand prescription coverage,
    pharmacy services, and medication costs in British Columbia.
  </p>

  <ExternalResource
    icon="💊"
    source="Government of British Columbia"
    title="Fair PharmaCare"
    description="Learn who is eligible for Fair PharmaCare and how to apply for assistance with prescription medication costs."
    href="https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/health/health-drug-coverage/pharmacare-for-bc-residents/fair-pharmacare-plan"
  />

  <ExternalResource
    icon="🌎"
    source="Government of Canada"
    title="Interim Federal Health Program (IFHP)"
    description="Information for refugees and protected persons about prescription drug coverage and other healthcare benefits."
    href="https://www.canada.ca/en/immigration-refugees-citizenship/services/refugees/help-within-canada/health-care.html"
  />

  <ExternalResource
    icon="🏥"
    source="BC Pharmacy Association"
    title="Find a Pharmacy"
    description="Locate community pharmacies throughout British Columbia."
    href="https://www.bcpharmacy.ca/"
  />
  <section>
  <h2>Paying for medications</h2>
  <p>
    Prescription medications are not always covered by MSP. Many British
    Columbians receive financial assistance through Fair PharmaCare, while
    eligible refugees and protected persons may receive coverage through the
    Interim Federal Health Program (IFHP).
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Your pharmacist can help</h2>
  <p>
    Pharmacists do much more than fill prescriptions. In British Columbia they
    can assess and prescribe medications for many minor illnesses, renew some
    prescriptions, administer many vaccines, provide contraception services,
    and answer questions about medications.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Getting a prescription</h2>
  <p>
    Bring your BC Services Card or other health coverage documents when you
    visit a pharmacy. If you need language support, ask the pharmacy staff if
    interpretation services are available.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Medication reviews</h2>
  <p>
    If you take several medications, ask your pharmacist about a medication
    review. They can explain how each medicine works, when to take it, possible
    side effects, and whether any medications may interact with each other.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Medication safety</h2>
  <p>
    Always take medications exactly as directed. Never share prescription
    medicines with others, and keep an up-to-date list of all prescription,
    over-the-counter, vitamin, and herbal products you use.
  </p>
</section>
<aside class="notice">
  <strong>Questions about your medication?</strong>
  <p>
    Your pharmacist is often the best person to ask about side effects,
    interactions, missed doses, or how to take a medication safely. If someone
    has a suspected overdose, collapses, has severe difficulty breathing, or
    develops a life-threatening allergic reaction, call 911 immediately.
  </p>
</aside>
</ResourceLayout>